{#
  One user's AmneziaWG configuration, in wg-quick's INI format.

  WHAT: the file a user imports into their client — their own keypair, the node as
        the single peer, and the obfuscation values that make this AmneziaWG.
  WHY:  this is the sketch the backlog carried as item 25 and could not build. The
        charter calls "how does a protocol with no URI form reach a phone in one link"
        the largest open question of phase 1, and the answer chosen by the owner
        (question Q11, option (b), 2026-08-09) is a per-client config file behind the
        same token. This template is that file.

        Note again what is NOT here: no logging directive of any kind, exactly as in
        server.j2. The absence is the feature.
  HOW:  rendered by render.py's ``render_client_config``. Every value is either an
        integer, a validated base64 key, or a parsed IP interface, so none can carry a
        newline and break out of its line — Params is strict for that reason, not for
        tidiness.

  Two things differ from server.j2, and both are deliberate:

  * **AllowedIPs is the whole internet here.** On the entry-to-leaf hop a wide route
    would hand the far end the right to source traffic as any address and would pull
    the node's own SSH session into the tunnel. On a user's device the whole point
    is that everything leaves through the node, so the full route is the correct
    value — the same field, an opposite answer, because the consumer is different.
    Both families, including the one no node in this fleet can carry —
    ``edge/egress.py``'s ``CLIENT_ROUTES`` is where that is argued.
  * **No ListenPort.** A client dials; it does not wait to be found. Pinning a source
    port would also make the user's traffic easier to correlate across networks.
#}
# hashi — generated for one device. Re-import after any rotation; the link never moves.
[Interface]
PrivateKey = {{ peer.private_key }}
Address = {{ peer.tunnel_address }}
MTU = {{ params.mtu }}
{% if resolvers %}
# The resolver this device asks inside the tunnel. Operator-supplied — this transport's
# own setting, or failing that the one on the node's record: there is no default, because
# a hardcoded resolver would be a fixed address this fleet could never rotate away from.
# It has to be here, because AllowedIPs below routes every address into the tunnel and
# that includes whichever resolver this device was using before.
DNS = {{ resolvers | join(", ") }}
{% else %}
# NO RESOLVER WAS SET FOR THIS NODE, and this line is the warning rather than a
# reassurance. AllowedIPs below routes EVERY address into the tunnel — that is the point
# of it — and the resolver this device already uses is one of those addresses. If it is a
# private one on the network this device is sitting in, a home router or an office
# server, it cannot be reached from the far end, and while this tunnel is up no name will
# resolve at all: addresses still work, names do not. Ask whoever gave you this file to
# set a resolver.
{% endif %}

# AmneziaWG obfuscation. These MUST match the node's values exactly — they are not
# preferences, they are the wire format, and one mismatched header means a tunnel that
# never comes up with nothing in any log to say why.
Jc = {{ params.junk_packet_count }}
Jmin = {{ params.junk_packet_min_size }}
Jmax = {{ params.junk_packet_max_size }}
H1 = {{ params.init_packet_magic_header }}
H2 = {{ params.response_packet_magic_header }}
H3 = {{ params.underload_packet_magic_header }}
H4 = {{ params.transport_packet_magic_header }}

[Peer]
# The node. One peer, never a list: a user's device talks to the exit it was given.
PublicKey = {{ params.public_key }}
PresharedKey = {{ peer.preshared_key }}
# Everything, in both families, and the second family is the deliberate half. This node's
# exit is IPv4 only, so IPv6 sent into this tunnel is dropped there and goes nowhere —
# which is the better of two bad answers. Leaving IPv6 out of this line would be faster
# and would send it out of this device's own connection instead, showing a real address
# to every site that has an IPv6 one. The cost of dropping it is a pause before a site
# that has both answers over IPv4.
AllowedIPs = {{ routes | join(", ") }}
Endpoint = {{ endpoint }}
# Keeps the UDP path open through NAT and stateful middleboxes. 25s is below the
# shortest common UDP idle timeout.
PersistentKeepalive = 25
